WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.547 --> 00:00:04.625
Tyrion: All hail Bran the
Broken, first of his name.

00:00:04.625 --> 00:00:07.000
All: All hail Bran the Broken.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.600
Narrator: It's official,
"Game of Thrones" has ended,

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.340
and the character who won it all

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:14.530
was one of the most overlooked
candidates for the crown.

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:17.930
Bran, the youngest Stark, has
no blood claim to the throne,

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:21.330
and he never seemed particularly
interested in ruling.

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:24.990
But if we look back, there were
some clues he'd end up king.

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:27.630
Here are 10 of them, and here's a warning:

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:29.580
Spoilers are coming.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.870
Bran's importance is established
early on in the series.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.030
In fact, he's the first major character

00:00:35.030 --> 00:00:36.250
we meet in the pilot.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:37.970
The first scene in Winterfell centers

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.760
on Bran practicing his archery

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.370
with the older Starks looking on.

00:00:41.370 --> 00:00:43.710
This introduction is
consistent with the novels,

00:00:43.710 --> 00:00:46.650
which open with a chapter
written from his point of view.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.280
Did author George R.R. Martin favor Bran

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.260
to end up on the throne all along?

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:52.780
It seems likely.

00:00:52.780 --> 00:00:54.790
One particular moment in the pilot

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.800
seems significant in retrospect.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.260
The camera cuts to young Bran

00:00:58.260 --> 00:01:01.020
during Ned Stark's recitation
of the execution oath.

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:02.717
Just as Ned says the line,

00:01:02.717 --> 00:01:05.187
"King of the Andals and the First Men."

00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:07.253
Ned: In the name of Robert
of the House Baratheon,

00:01:07.253 --> 00:01:08.359
the First of His Name...

00:01:08.359 --> 00:01:09.587
Jon: Don't look away.

00:01:09.587 --> 00:01:10.893
Ned: King of the Andals
and the First Men...

00:01:10.893 --> 00:01:11.840
Jon: Father will know if you do.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:14.140
Narrator: It seems they
left it deliberately unclear

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.130
whether we should focus on
Bran or Jon in that shot.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.150
That's a tactic the show often uses

00:01:19.150 --> 00:01:21.190
to subvert our expectations.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:23.290
For example, after Hardhome, it seemed

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:25.140
that the Night King was after Jon,

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:29.057
when in fact it was Bran
that he really wanted.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.600
Based on the Night
King's fixation on Bran,

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:33.920
maybe we should have known

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.840
that he was destined for the throne.

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:38.650
As Bran explained before
the Battle of Winterfell,

00:01:38.650 --> 00:01:41.410
the Night King's goal
was to erase this world

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:42.730
in its entirety.

00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:45.760
To do so, he had to erase Bran first.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:47.230
In view of the show's ending,

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.590
some fans believe the
Night King was dead set

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:51.520
on destroying Bran because he knew

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:53.300
he'd become Westeros' ruler

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:56.080
and a strong, competent ruler at that.

00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:59.180
It seems significant that
Bran is also the first person

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.300
we see targeted by Jaime Lannister,

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:04.123
the man infamously known as "Kingslayer."

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:08.630
In the finale, when Tyrion
makes his impassioned case

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:10.290
in front of Westeros' leaders,

00:02:10.290 --> 00:02:13.640
it's clear he has a strong
appreciation for Bran.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:15.330
Apparently, it's reciprocated

00:02:15.330 --> 00:02:17.460
because Bran makes Tyrion his hand

00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:21.260
despite his spotty track
record as Daenerys' adviser.

00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:23.230
In the events leading up to this moment,

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:25.430
it might have been easy
to overlook the friendship

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:27.860
between Bran and Tyrion, especially since

00:02:27.860 --> 00:02:30.660
they've only interacted a
handful of times on-screen,

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.140
but their special bond actually dates back

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.160
to the fourth episode of the series.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.180
Following Bran's injury,
Tyrion traveled all the way

00:02:38.180 --> 00:02:41.620
to Winterfell, bringing designs
for a customized saddle.

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.620
Bran: Will I really be able to ride?

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:44.920
Tyrion: You will.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.700
Robb: Is this some kind of trick?

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:48.100
Why do you want to help him?

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.300
Tyrion: I've a tender spot in my heart

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:52.500
for cripples, bastards, and broken things.

00:02:52.500 --> 00:02:54.300
Narrator: Tyrion's
words inspired the title

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:57.960
of that episode in season
one, as well as the moniker

00:02:57.960 --> 00:03:00.913
of Bran the Broken that he coins later on.

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:03.980
Tyrion and Bran's relationship picks up

00:03:03.980 --> 00:03:05.210
right where they left off

00:03:05.210 --> 00:03:07.520
in the second episode of season eight.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:09.350
After the battle-strategy meeting,

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:12.133
everyone leaves the room
except the two of them.

00:03:12.133 --> 00:03:14.640
Tyrion pulls up a chair and asks Bran

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.880
to recount the story of his travels.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.690
The writer of this episode, Bryan Cogman,

00:03:19.690 --> 00:03:23.040
tipped us off to the significance
of the Bran-Tyrion bond

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.037
in an interview back in April.

00:03:25.037 --> 00:03:28.037
"Tyrion does have a connection
with Bran," he said.

00:03:28.037 --> 00:03:30.887
"Tyrion's a student of history
and I think him wanting

00:03:30.887 --> 00:03:34.277
to talk to Bran is about
him trying to understand...

00:03:34.277 --> 00:03:37.650
Obviously, Bran's had a
very unique experience."

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.970
This would explain why later
Tyrion is so ready to vouch

00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:43.860
for Bran in light of his
incredible personal journey

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:45.940
to becoming the Three-Eyed Raven.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:48.920
Tyrion approaches Bran
again two episodes later

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:50.850
at the victory celebration following

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:52.490
the Battle of Winterfell.

00:03:52.490 --> 00:03:55.300
He compliments Bran's wheelchair,
saying it's even better

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.460
than the saddle he designed
back in season one.

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:01.290
According to Bran, his
chair is based on the one

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.050
Daeron Targaryen built for
his nephew 120 years ago.

00:04:06.050 --> 00:04:09.930
Based on the timeframe, he
must be referencing Daeron II,

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:11.820
also known as Daeron the Good.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.320
His familiarity with that
history impresses Tyrion,

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.510
who told Joffrey back in
season four that Daeron's story

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:22.026
should be required reading
for any future ruler.

00:04:22.026 --> 00:04:23.184
Joffrey: A book?

00:04:23.184 --> 00:04:24.626
Tyrion: "The Lives of the Four Kings,"

00:04:24.626 --> 00:04:26.290
Grand Maester Kaeth's
history of the reigns

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:30.210
of Daeron the Young
Dragon, Baelor the Blessed,

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:32.363
Aegon the Unworthy, and Daeron the Good.

00:04:32.363 --> 00:04:34.510
A book every king should read.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.470
Narrator: Tyrion says
Bran's historical knowledge

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:39.010
will serve him well as Lord of Winterfell,

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.520
but he's surprised to
learn Bran has no intention

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.930
of holding that title.

00:04:42.930 --> 00:04:44.800
At this point, it's entirely possible

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.660
Bran had already foreseen
himself on the throne,

00:04:47.660 --> 00:04:49.620
in which case his
reaction would make sense

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.420
on multiple levels.

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.670
Bran: I don't really want anymore.

00:04:53.670 --> 00:04:55.900
Narrator: If he expected
to be crowned king,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:58.000
why would he take on a lordship?

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.840
In episode six, he does
imply that he'd known

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.000
about his destiny beforehand.

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:05.220
As for Daenerys, it's safe to assume

00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:08.380
Bran knew her fate well
before the rest of us.

00:05:08.380 --> 00:05:10.510
In a vision back in season four,

00:05:10.510 --> 00:05:12.710
he saw the shadow of a dragon flying

00:05:12.710 --> 00:05:15.340
over the rooftops of King's Landing.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.710
When he was fleeing the cave
with Meera in season six,

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:22.360
that ominous shot recurred
in a longer dream sequence

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:24.750
that also included images of Daenerys

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.790
and of a full-grown Drogon in the sky.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.610
These were intercut with shots of Aerys II

00:05:30.610 --> 00:05:35.000
and his green wildfire, connecting
the Mad King of the past

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:37.340
with the Mad Queen of the near future.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:39.290
The question is whether Bran shared

00:05:39.290 --> 00:05:41.230
this information with anyone.

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:44.220
Some fans have theorized that
his conversation with Tyrion

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:47.520
in episode four continued
after the camera cut away

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:50.090
and that during that time, Bran warned him

00:05:50.090 --> 00:05:52.470
about Daenerys' imminent descent.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.600
It wouldn't be the first
time the Three-Eyed Raven

00:05:54.600 --> 00:05:56.460
issued warnings off-screen.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.430
The season seven finale revealed

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.480
that Bran had been advising his sisters

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.640
about Littlefinger's schemes.

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:05.540
Did he confide similarly in Tyrion?

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:07.580
If so, that would explain the certainty

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:09.700
with which Tyrion spoke to Jaime

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:11.893
about the outcome of the impending battle.

00:06:12.790 --> 00:06:14.550
Jamie: It's not impossible
that she'll win.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:15.383
Tyrion: She won't.

00:06:15.383 --> 00:06:18.680
Jamie: If the three dragons are
dead, she's evened the odds.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.240
Tyrion: The city will fall tomorrow.

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:22.933
There won't be an Iron
Fleet for much longer.

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:25.590
Do it!

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:28.160
If you don't, you'll
never see Cersei again.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:29.980
Narrator: Maybe Tyrion mistakenly thought

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:31.890
he could change the inevitable

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:35.347
by issuing fierce warnings
to all the important players.

00:06:35.347 --> 00:06:36.390
Tyrion: Her followers will abandon her

00:06:36.390 --> 00:06:37.720
if they know the war is lost.

00:06:37.720 --> 00:06:38.863
Give them that chance.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.360
If the city surrenders,
they will ring the bells

00:06:42.360 --> 00:06:43.840
and raise the gates, please,

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.760
if you hear them ringing the
bells, call off the attack.

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.033
Swear to me, escape.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:51.740
The two of you, together.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:53.930
Narrator: Bran's detached
attitude toward power

00:06:53.930 --> 00:06:56.020
may be the result of foresight,

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.410
or it may just reflect
the state of enlightenment

00:06:58.410 --> 00:07:00.670
he's reached as the Three-Eyed Raven.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:03.560
Either way, the show hints
that the kind of indifference

00:07:03.560 --> 00:07:06.350
Bran exhibits is actually an asset.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:09.260
This idea is referenced in
season eight, episode four,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:13.460
when Tyrion and Varys are
discussing Jon's fitness to rule.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.600
Tyrion: He doesn't want the throne.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:16.840
Varys: Have you considered the best ruler

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.250
might be someone who doesn't want to rule?

00:07:19.250 --> 00:07:21.410
Narrator: It's likely
that Varys' observation

00:07:21.410 --> 00:07:23.220
made Tyrion think of his exchange

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:25.570
with Bran earlier in that episode.

00:07:25.570 --> 00:07:29.160
Perhaps it was at this moment
that the idea was planted

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:32.743
in Tyrion's head that Bran
should be crowned king.

00:07:33.580 --> 00:07:34.990
What do you think?

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:37.700
Did the show hint at
Bran's destiny all along,

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:39.940
or did the ending feel out of the blue?

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:42.100
And did we miss any major clues?

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.023
Let us know in the comments.

